"Let $S_k$ be the symmetric group on $k$ letters. For a fixed finite set of patterns $\\subset S_k$, let $C_n(\\mathcal{F})$ be the number of permutations in $S_n$ avoiding every pattern in $\\mathcal{F}$. An integer sequence $\\{a_n\\}$ is **P-recursive** if it satisfies a nontrivial linear recurrence with polynomial coefficients, namely\n\n$$\nq_0(n)a_n+q_1(n)a_{n-1}+\\cdots+q_k(n)a_{n-k}=0\n$$\n\nfor some $q_i(x)\\in\\mathbb Z[x]$. **Noonan–Zeilberger conjecture.** For every fixed finite set of patterns $\\mathcal{F}\\subset S_k$, the sequence $\\{C_n(\\mathcal{F})\\}$ is P-recursive. This was a longstanding conjecture supported by many positive results for special pattern sets, but the paper disproves it."